column2:
What is this "Stack Overflow" you speak of?<p>Oh yes. Now I remember. I used to visit the site <i>every day</i>.<p>I love helping my fellow developers and answering their questions whenever I can.<p>My reputation there is 29,020, and I've contributed 645 quality answers.<p>I used to brag about this.<p>Now I had to think a bit to remember SO still exists.<p>My last answer was on May 28, 2021, over five years ago.<p>Obviously it wasn't LLMs that drove me away. And I'm not sure what it was.<p>Was it the culture, as others here pointed out? Or did I just get busy with other things?<p>---<p>Update: Now I think I remember what muted my enthusiasm.<p>Some of you older programmers may have used jQuery.<p>There is a common convention to use $ as the first character of a variable name that is a jQuery object:<p><pre><code>  var $foo = $('#foo');
</code></pre>
One of my few claims to fame is that I invented that convention and promoted it on the early jQuery mailing list.<p>Someone asked about this:<p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/205853/why-would-a-javascript-variable-start-with-a-dollar-sign/48558883" rel="nofollow">https://stackoverflow.com/questions/205853/why-would-a-javas...</a><p>The top-rated answer, with 1542 votes, is a good summary.<p>The second answer, with 278 votes, is as irrelevant as an answer can be.<p>I'm in third place with 71 votes. I gave the story behind the naming convention, because I like people to know the "how and why".<p>It gets better! This question was marked as a duplicate of another one. That other question has a wild variety of answers, but none that does what I tried to do: explain the reason and background for the convention.<p>Ah well. It was fun while it lasted.
